A wafer fab consumes four families of inputs:
- photoresists and their building blocks, used in lithography;
- ultra-pure wet chemicals for cleaning and etching, such as hydrogen peroxide, isopropyl alcohol, ammonia water, sulfuric acid and hydrofluoric acid;
- electronic specialty gases, such as NF3, WF6, silane, helium and neon;
- bulk high-purity nitrogen, oxygen and argon.
The Sumitomo-Tata Electronics MoU names exactly this wet-chemicals and photoresist basket for the Dholera fab (S6). Fujifilm's plan adds chemical-mechanical planarisation (CMP) slurries and surface-conditioning materials (S21). IndexBox notes that India makes bulk nitrogen, oxygen and argon locally but largely imports the critical etch and deposition gases (S5). Packaging (ATMP/OSAT) plants mainly use inert and specialty gases, to prevent oxidation, manage heat and test package integrity (S16).
